    "current_example": {
      "math_academy_id": "e-9525",
      "problem": "Calculate $\\displaystyle\\int_C \\,\\textrm d x,$ where $C=C_1 \\cup C_2$ is the path comprising of two line segments, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14119854/assets/3d408de0d50e2688aa0b35d8af39b6847cfdf21d5ec583320f0204384304fbc8.png)",
      "worked_solution": "The line integral of unity with respect to $x$ along a path $C$ is equal to the net change in $x$ between the terminal and initial points:\n\n$$\n\\int_C \\,\\textrm d x = x_2 - x_1,\n$$\n\nwhere $x_2$ is the $x$-coordinate of the terminal point, and $x_1$ is the $x$-coordinate of the initial point.\n\nIn our case, $x_2 = -3$ and $x_1 = 3.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\int_C \\,\\textrm d x = x_2 - x_1 = (-3) - 3 = -6.\n$$",
      "html_path": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14119854/observations/step-e9525-efc25dc626d26a298ca9.html",
